@title Replacing Jenkins
@subtitle Comparing CI providers for the firmware and cloud repos
@author Developer Experience, Ridgeback Robotics

kpi Build minutes | 38,000/month
kpi Engineers | 120
kpi Special needs | ARM + HIL lab | some jobs

Some jobs need ARM runners, and firmware tests need our hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) lab. The options are GitHub Actions, CircleCI and Buildkite.

# Comparison
table = Provider,Median build time (min),ARM runners,Self-hosted agents,Monthly cost; GitHub Actions,14.2,yes,yes,"$2,860"; CircleCI,11.8,yes,yes,"$3,420"; Buildkite,9.6,via our own agents,yes (required),"$2,150 + our compute"

# Benchmarks
Median build time for our three reference pipelines on each provider.

data bench = pipeline,GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite; firmware-build,21.4,17.9,13.2; cloud-api-tests,9.8,8.1,7.4; docs-site,3.1,2.7,2.9
chart bar bench unit=min title="Median build time by pipeline"

Buildkite is fastest on the two heavy pipelines; on docs-site the three are within half a minute of each other.

# Pros and cons
## GitHub Actions
- Pro: lives next to our code, no extra tool to learn.
- Pro: hosted ARM runners and self-hosted support.
- Con: slowest median build at 14.2 minutes.

## CircleCI
- Pro: faster than GitHub Actions on every pipeline.
- Pro: hosted ARM runners.
- Con: most expensive at $3,420 a month.

## Buildkite
- Pro: fastest median build at 9.6 minutes.
- Pro: lowest license cost.
- Pro: self-hosted agents fit the HIL lab.
- Con: we pay for and run our own compute.
- Con: we patch the runners.

# Recommendation
! Choose **Buildkite**. The hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents anyway, and Buildkite is the fastest and cheapest option on license cost.

!! Buildkite moves runner patching onto the DevEx on-call, estimated at 6 hours a month.
